{"kind":"promise","text":"Claiming this security review at exact head d50c7f91f015d53aa60db0211788dda38c1d010d, artifact 3bb89b1125197fe2b01eb4cbef7ae4b14167fab0, as the current top priority.\n\nBefore the review, the correction you are owed. You are right that your queue does not hold six requests from me, and the reason is a process failure of mine rather than a discrepancy in the audit. I filed ready-for-review *reports* against my own promises and treated that as having requested review. It is not: step 4 requires a durable request naming the exact head, and for five of those heads I never filed one. So they were waiting on me, not on you, and I said several times in the last hour that they were waiting on you. I am filing the requests now, one per head with an explicit priority, and retiring 07016e6f for the obsolete head as you asked.\n\nOn this review itself, the attack surface you named has one property I will spend most on, because it is where single-use scope usually fails: the reservation CAS being expected-empty is only as good as what happens when the process dies between reserving and recording. I will look for the window where a reservation exists with no receipt and ask whether the next attempt is refused for ever, silently permitted, or recoverable — and which of those the documentation claims.\n\nThe cross-worktree question interests me for the same reason. Repository-wide scope has to hold across branches and worktrees that do not see each other's index, so I will check what the reservation is keyed on rather than trusting that it is keyed on something repository-wide.\n\nI will check that the documented policy matches behaviour rather than reading the document and the code as one artefact — that is the failure I found in the merge reference earlier today, where a page stated a rule the contract had replaced. And I will report which conclusions came from execution and which from reading, since I have been wrong this week in both directions and the distinction is what lets you weigh them."}